00:00:28.635 --> 00:00:30.041
JONATHAN KOLA: Yep, let's do it.

00:00:30.041 --> 00:00:32.005
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.130
On GCP, the nodes on
your Kubernetes cluster

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:43.150
could either all be in one
zone, or across multiple zones

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:43.694
in a region.

00:00:43.694 --> 00:00:46.360
And on these nodes, you could be
running multiple services which

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.820
you need to expose to the
outside world somehow.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.430
And for this, you would
use an Ingress resource.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:54.460
And on GCP, we implement that
using the Google Cloud Load

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:55.380
Balancer.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.240
It allows you to map HTTP
requests or particular service

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.870
or services running
on your nodes.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.700
Now, with Multi-cluster Ingress,
it is actually a new feature.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.820
It allows you to
route your traffic

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.150
across multiple services
across multiple nodes.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.810
So it's a really powerful
feature, a really cool feature.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:12.540
So let's jump into the demo.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.790
I'm in my console
now, and specifically

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.020
in the Google Kubernetes
Engine section.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.480
And you can see, I have two
clusters here that I've already

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.240
provisioned, cluster
1 and cluster 2,

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.540
running in US East
4 and Europe West 1.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.090
Diving into one of the clusters,
we can take a quick look

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.780
at the configuration.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:32.640
It is a basic default
setup, so three nodes

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.390
running Kubernetes 1.9.7.

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.120
These are essentially the
defaults, so nothing too fancy

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:39.734
going on here.

00:01:39.734 --> 00:01:41.400
We can also take a
look at the workloads

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.990
that run on the cluster.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.460
And we see we have
two workloads.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.380
These are two
deployments running

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.020
on the two different clusters.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:49.890
And they are both
called Zone Printer.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.920
If we dive into one of
the applications, one

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.850
of the deployments, we can take
a look at what the UI shows us.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:58.050
It shows us basic
metrics, but what's

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.901
going on with those
workloads running

00:01:59.901 --> 00:02:01.650
on the cluster, or one
particular cluster,

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:02.440
in this case.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:04.560
And we can see by
setup that it's just

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.480
comprised of three
different parts,

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.490
all serving the
same application.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:11.875
Now, we've also actually
provisioned an Ingress resource

00:02:11.875 --> 00:02:13.500
beforehand, which
we'll take a look at.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.440
And if you remember, an
Ingress resource on Google

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.360
is provisioned using
the Google Cloud Load

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.000
Balancer, which is what
we're going to navigate to.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.950
And if we dive into our
Google Cloud Load Balancer

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:29.059
implementation, we can
actually grab the IP address

00:02:29.059 --> 00:02:30.600
that's going to be
our [INAUDIBLE] IP

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.120
address that we'll
actually use to communicate

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.260
with the services.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.260
So we can grab that IP
address and go to our browser

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.210
and redirect ourselves
to that address.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:40.840
And this is what
we're greeted with.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.530
We have the US flag and
a nice welcome message

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:44.820
from Ashburn, Virginia.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.250
That is the exact place
where our cluster is running.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.860
It's in the US East 4 A zone.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.010
Now, what we want to
do next is simulate

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.260
what would happen if
one of our clusters,

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:59.540
or the applications running
on those clusters, falls over.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.430
Underneath the
hood, GKE clusters

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:03.020
are actually Managed
Instance groups.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.860
So we can navigate to the
Managed Instance group page

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.440
and actually simulate
what this kind of scenario

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:09.920
would look like.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.504
So if we set Restart
Event to actually restart

00:03:14.504 --> 00:03:16.920
all of the nodes, this will
kill what's running right now.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.460
The nodes will be drained.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:23.060
And that cluster, for a brief
period, will be unavailable.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:24.160
So we've done that.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.780
Hopefully, in a few seconds,
we will have our cluster coming

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:28.569
offline, but soon
to be restarted.

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:30.110
So let's navigate
back to the browser

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:32.650
and see what
actually happens now.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:35.580
And after a few
seconds, success.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.520
We've been redirected
to the Belgium zone.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.730
That's Europe West 1, where
our second cluster is running

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.330
with the same application.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:44.490
So this is pretty cool.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.700
In a normal scenario,
wherever your users

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.450
are trying to access
the application from,

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.490
they would actually be
routed to the zone that's

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:51.540
closest to them.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.027
This is a powerful
Multi-cluster Ingress.

00:03:54.027 --> 00:03:55.110
So what do you think, Rui?

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:55.890
RUI COSTA: That was
amazing, Jonathan.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:56.590
Thank you.

